<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        An account is anything with a balance: a chequing or savings account, a credit card, a line of credit, a mortgage or
        other loan, or an investment account. Add one for each you want to follow; every transaction belongs to one.
    </p>
    <HelpClip Name="accounts-add" Caption="Adding a credit card with what's owed on it." />

    <h3>Adding an account</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>On <a href="accounts">Accounts</a>, choose <span class="help-ui">Add account</span>.</li>
        <li>Give it a <span class="help-ui">Name</span> you'll recognise, such as <em>Everyday chequing</em> or <em>Visa Infinite</em>.</li>
        <li>Pick the <span class="help-ui">Institution</span> (start typing for a list of Canadian banks and credit unions) and the <span class="help-ui">Type</span>.</li>
        <li>Pick its <span class="help-ui">Currency</span>: <span class="help-ui">CAD</span> for most accounts, <span class="help-ui">USD</span>
            for a US-dollar account. Its balance is in that currency.</li>
        <li>Optionally enter the <span class="help-ui">Last 4 digits</span>, to tell two cards from the same bank apart. Only those four
            digits are ever stored, never the full number.</li>
        <li>Enter the <span class="help-ui">Current balance</span> (for a credit card, line of credit or loan, the <em>Amount owed</em>, as a
            positive number), or leave it and let an import set it. Lines of credit and cards also take a <span class="help-ui">Credit limit</span>.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Save</span>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>The type matters</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Chequing, savings, GIC and investment</strong> accounts are assets: their balances add to net worth.</li>
        <li><strong>Credit cards, lines of credit, mortgages and loans</strong> are what you owe: their balances come off net worth.</li>
        <li>Moving money between your own accounts, and paying a credit card, are <em>transfers</em>, not spending or income, so
            they don't count twice. See <a href="help?a=categorizing">Categories and review</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>US-dollar accounts</h3>
    <p>
        The <span class="help-ui">Currency</span> column shows each account's currency, and <span class="help-ui">Balance</span> is in it
        (every amount in LoonieBooks names its currency: CA$ for Canadian dollars, US$ for US dollars, the code for others such
        as EUR; from a million up, amounts are shortened to two decimals, so CA$3.57M is about CA$3,570,000). When any account isn't in Canadian dollars, <span class="help-ui">In CAD</span> shows every balance in
        Canadian dollars at the ledger's exchange rate (point at one to see the rate); the totals above each table, and net worth on
        the dashboard, add these up. The rate is the Bank of Canada's latest, fetched when the page opens (only the currency code is
        sent), or your own, set on <a href="holdings">Holdings</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        For an investment account, holdings whose report doesn't say their currency are read as the account's. Changing the
        account's currency with <span class="help-ui">Edit</span> moves those holdings to the new currency, and a balance that was their
        total becomes their total in it. That fixes holdings imported while the account was still set to CAD.
    </p>

    <h3>Keeping balances right</h3>
    <p>
        When an import includes a running balance (most bank pages and statements show one), the import page offers to
        <span class="help-ui">Set account balance</span> to the latest one, with its date. The <span class="help-ui">As of</span> column shows
        when each balance was last set. You can also correct it any time with <span class="help-ui">Edit</span>.
    </p>

    <h3>Real estate and equity</h3>
    <p>
        Under <span class="help-ui">Real estate</span>, <span class="help-ui">Add property</span> records a home or other property: a
        name (such as its street address), the <span class="help-ui">City or area</span> it's in, a <span class="help-ui">Value</span>
        (a purchase price, appraisal or assessment) and the date that value is from. Tick the mortgages and home equity lines
        secured by it, or choose the property under <span class="help-ui">Secured by</span> when you edit the loan. A loan is
        secured by one property at a time; a TD FlexLine's term and revolving portions can all point at the same home.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>The table shows each property's value, what's owed on its loans, its <strong>equity</strong> (value less what's owed) and
            its <strong>loan-to-value</strong> (what's owed as a share of the value).</li>
        <li>A property's value counts towards net worth on the dashboard; its loans are already counted as what you owe.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Keeping a property's value current</h3>
    <p>
        A property's value is carried forward by its area's house price index: the CREA MLS® Home Price Index, which LoonieBooks
        gets from ownquity.com. Pick the area from the list under <span class="help-ui">City or area</span>, or type the city
        (<em>Oakville</em> finds <em>Oakville Milton</em>); the hint under the field says which index the value will follow. The value
        moves by the index's change since the month it's from: for example, $800,000 in March 2019 with the index at 280 then and
        350 now is shown as $1,000,000. The note under each value shows the change and the latest month the index has.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>The whole index, every area and month, is downloaded and the area is found on your device, so nothing about your
            properties is sent. LoonieBooks checks for a newer index when Accounts or the dashboard opens, at most every six hours.
            The index is published monthly, so the latest month is usually one or two behind.</li>
        <li>When there's no index for the name you gave, the value stays as entered; the note says so.</li>
        <li>When you have a newer figure, such as a fresh appraisal or property assessment, <span class="help-ui">Edit</span> the
            property and change its value and date: the index then carries it from that month.</li>
        <li>These are estimates for your own records, not appraisals. A single home can do better or worse than its area's index.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Other buttons</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Import</span> on a row opens the import page with that account already chosen.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Holdings</span> takes its place on an investment account and opens its holdings; importing the
            monthly statement or the broker's holdings report keeps the balance up to date. See
            <a href="help?a=holdings">Investment holdings</a>.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Delete</span> asks once more, then deletes the account <strong>and all its transactions</strong>. To
            take back one wrong import instead, use undo; see <a href="help?a=undo-duplicates">Undo an import</a>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
